===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{senseid|en|pertaining}} Pertaining to [[Abraham]], the [[patriarch]]. {{defdate|from the early 19th century.}}<ref>{{R:SOED5|page=7}}</ref>
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1832|volume=4|author=Issac Cullimore|magazine=The Morning watch: or, Quarterly journal on prophecy, and theological review|title=Criteria for Determining the Accuracy of Scripture Chronology|passage=[T]he Noachic and '''Abrahamic''' churches are connected by Shem, and the other long-lived patriarchs, who existed before the apostasy of Noah's posterity, and survived it}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1896|journal=The Methodist review|page=66|title=Spiritual Development of St. Paul|author=James S. Kennedy|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=YTAuAAAAYAAJ&printsec=titlepage#PPA66,M1|passage=Paul's faith was at this crisis in his spiritual illumination more '''Abrahamic''' than Christlike in its character.}}
# {{senseid|en|descended}} {{lb|en|of a|_|religion}} Descended from the [[religious]] [[tradition]] of [[Abraham]].
#: {{ux|en|Judaism, Christianity, Islam and the Baháʼí Faith are all '''Abrahamic''' religions.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|quotee=Jacques Derrida|page=121|passage=[The ''messianic''] does not belong properly to any '''Abrahamic''' religion (even if I may here continue “entre nous” for essential reasons of language and of place, of culture, and of provisional rhetorical and historical strategy of which I will speak later, to give to it names inscribed by the '''Abrahamic''' religions).|title=Derrida and Religion: Other Testaments|author=Yvonne Sherwood; Kevin Hart}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Religion: Beyond a Concept|author=Hent De Vries|year=2007|page=123|isbn=0823227251|passage=Most anthropologists, myself at the forefront, are doubtless incredibly naive about the nature of Christianity and provincial with respect to the depth and riches of '''Abrahamic'''-based theory for the analysis of religious phenomena more broadly no less than for philosophy.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|title=The Borders of Islam|isbn=0231154224|author=Stig Jarle Hansen; Atle Mesøy; Tuncay Kardas|page=158|passage=Christianity, not Islam, was the first of the '''Abrahamic''' cults to come to the Sudan.}}

====Usage notes====
* Not all religions that revere Abraham are considered Abrahamic. The term always covers [[Christianity]], [[Islam]] and [[Judaism]], and often includes the [[Baháʼí Faith]]. Other faiths descended from these religions, such as [[Rastafari]] and [[Mandaeism]], are sometimes not described as Abrahamic even if they consider Abraham holy.<ref>{{cite-web|work=Religious Tolerance|publisher=Ontario Consultants on Religious Tolerance|title=Meanings of ambiguous terms beginning with the letters "A" to "R"|titleurl=http://www.religioustolerance.org/alt_mean1.htm|accessdate=2012-06-09}}</ref>

